HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC) is seeking to engage with suppliers to understand the current capabilities of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and related technologies in supporting the analysis, design and implementation of tax legislation.
HMRC's strategic ambition is to deliver a tax system that is simpler for customers, easier to administer and more resilient to future change. Recent exploratory work has indicated that AI and companion technologies have potential to support tax simplification and future policy design, although further evidence is required to assess available solutions, their maturity and the feasibility of their application within HMRC.
We want to understand how emerging technologies could support the tax policy lifecycle, from analysing complexity in the existing system and designing simpler policy and legislation, through to expressing and implementing tax rules more consistently across operational and digital systems.
This engagement is intended to inform HMRC's understanding of available capabilities and does not represent a commitment to procure any goods or services or to implement any specific solution. However, its aim is to establish whether sufficiently mature capabilities exist to justify one or more bounded HMRC test and learn exercises.
As part of this exploratory activity, we want to understand current market capabilities, emerging technologies and supplier experience in two areas:
Workstream 1: AI and Tax Simplification
Problem statement: How can HMRC use AI to improve the way we analyse, design and simplify tax rules, and can we do so in a way that delivers sufficient benefit to justify the additional technology and resource involved?
HMRC wishes to understand whether AI-enabled tools can assist in analysing large volumes of legislation, guidance and related materials to identify potential opportunities for simplification, and whether AI can make existing complexity easier for users to understand and navigate without requiring legislative change.
Areas of interest include, but are not limited to:
•	Identifying ambiguity, duplication and unnecessary complexity within legislation;
•	Mapping legislative dependencies, cross-references and interactions;
•	Highlighting areas where legislation may be difficult to navigate, interpret or administer;
•	Supporting legislative review and simplification exercises;
•	Assisting officials in developing options for legislative reform; and
•	Generating insights that may not be readily apparent through conventional manual review.
In addition, we are also interested in proposals which could:
•	Present complex legislation and rules into simpler, accessible guidance or explanations, while accurately reflecting the underlying legislative requirements;
HMRC is interested in understanding existing products, technologies and emerging capabilities, including examples of deployment within government, regulatory or legal environments.
Workstream 2: Rules as Code
Problem statement: How can HMRC use Rules as Code (RaC) to improve the way we design, implement and maintain tax rules, and can AI reduce the effort and complexity involved in creating, translating and maintaining machine-consumable rules?
For HMRC, RaC offers the potential to support clearer rulemaking, both by applying the discipline when developing new policy and legislation and retrospectively to existing rules, while strengthening the connection between policy, law and delivery. Around 90% of digital returns received by HMRC are submitted using third-party software, across a market of approximately 3,000 software providers. Publishing machine-consumable rules could provide a common, testable and verifiable basis from which software providers, agents and businesses can implement tax rules, rather than each independently translating the underlying legislation and guidance.
Our work through the OECD's RaC project has found that translating natural-language rules into machine-consumable representations can also help expose ambiguities, unintended interactions and missing information. Therefore, applying the discipline of RaC could also help identify opportunities to clarify and simplify tax rules.
While RaC is not itself AI, creating, testing and maintaining machine-consumable rules can be resource-intensive and require significant specialist input. We want to understand whether AI can reduce this burden by supporting the translation, modelling, testing and maintenance of machine-consumable rules.
Areas of interest include:
•	The role of AI in supporting the extraction of legislative rules, concepts and decision logic;
•	Translation into structured, machine-consumable representations;
•	Identification of ambiguities, assumptions and data requirements;
•	Testing, validating, and maintaining machine-consumable rule sets;
•	Version and change management as legislation and interpretations evolve; and
•	Interoperability between tools, standards and systems.
HMRC is particularly interested in approaches that maintain legal accuracy, transparency and expert policy, legal and operational oversight, rather than replacing human judgement.
Outcomes of Interest
Our aim is to establish whether sufficiently mature capabilities exist to justify one or more bounded HMRC test-and-learn exercises and, if so, which problems, approaches and technologies offer the strongest basis for testing. The evidence gathered through this engagement will help HMRC determine where further experimentation could add most value and shape the scope and design of any subsequent test-and-learn activity.
Through this engagement HMRC wishes to understand:
•	The extent to which suppliers believe AI can support legislative simplification and RaC adoption in practice and the maturity of the market;
•	The current capability of available products and services;
•	Examples of successful deployment in legislative, regulatory or policy environments;
•	Delivery models and implementation approaches;
•	Limitations, risks and dependencies; and
•	Potential approaches for future test and learn activity.
This engagement is intended to inform HMRC's understanding of available capabilities and does not represent a commitment to procure any goods or services or to implement any specific solution.
While this is being undertaken as a preliminary market engagement exercise, HMRC is also interested in hearing from the wider expert community, including academics, researchers and other organisations with relevant expertise in AI, tax simplification and Rules as Code.

To support the development of the next phase of regulations, Employment and Social Development Canada (ESDC) is launching this RFI to gather information from digital technology vendors and suppliers on their current accessibility-related capabilities, practices, implementation challenges, and readiness with respect to artificial intelligence (AI) systems, software, and hardware.
The RFI seeks information on:
•	Current accessibility capabilities, policies, and practices relating to software, hardware, and AI systems;
•	Conformance with relevant accessibility standards and specifications for software, hardware, and AI systems;
•	Approaches to incorporating accessibility throughout the product lifecycle, including design, development, testing, deployment, and procurement;
•	Barriers, implementation challenges, and considerations associated with achieving accessibility; and,
•	Organizational readiness to meet potential future accessibility requirements.
The information collected through this process will help inform the development of the next phase of Digital Technologies Accessibility Regulations under the ACA.
